Binder and cgroup freezer regression test
========================================

binder_freezer creates a private BinderFS instance and a cgroup, then
exchanges real transactions between a caller and a context manager.
It checks incoming and outgoing pending-transaction accounting,
BINDER_FREEZE refusal while a reply is pending, BINDER_GET_FROZEN_INFO,
synchronous calls to a frozen process, queued one-way calls, thawing,
and SIGKILL with work still queued. Deferred Binder cleanup must finish
before the private mount can be removed.

Run as root in an isolated test VM with /proc and /sys mounted and a
writable /tmp. Required configuration: ANDROID_BINDER_IPC,
ANDROID_BINDERFS, ANDROID_BINDER_DEVICES including "binder", CGROUPS,
UNIX, TMPFS and DEVTMPFS. Mount cgroup v2 first. The optional argument
is its mount point; the default is /cg2. The test creates a child group
under that mount, without moving the caller out of its existing group.

Build from the kernel source directory:

    binder_work="$HOME/TEMP/binder-selftest"
    mkdir -p "$binder_work"
    aarch64-linux-gnu-gcc -static -O2 -Wall -Wextra -Werror \
        tools/testing/selftests/binder/binder_freezer.c \
        -o "$binder_work/binder_freezer"

In the test VM:

    mkdir -p /cg2
    mount -t cgroup2 none /cg2
    ./binder_freezer /cg2

A complete run prints 25 TAP results. Exit status is 0 on success,
1 for failed checks, 2 for setup/protocol failures, or 4 when root or
BinderFS is unavailable. The test times out after 120 seconds. An
aborted test can leave its temporary mount, cgroup or child behind;
discard the isolated test VM after such a run.

Enable PROVE_LOCKING and DEBUG_VM in the test kernel and inspect its
log too. Intentional failures print Binder diagnostics for EAGAIN and
BR_FROZEN_REPLY. Killing the frozen server deliberately discards its
queued transactions. Outstanding-transaction warnings, lockdep reports,
use-after-free reports and kernel oopses are not expected.

This validates the kernel interfaces. It does not test Android's task
profiles, SELinux policy or actual cached-app retention.
